QUESTIONS = [
    # CH50 SPSC L1 (+30)
    {"text":"Under which Part of the Constitution are the provisions regarding State Public Service Commissions (SPSCs) found?","options":["Part XI","Part XII","Part XIV","Part XV"],"correct_answer":"Part XIV","explanation":"Articles 315 to 323 in Part XIV of the Constitution deal with the composition, appointment, and removal of members along with the independence, powers, and functions of the SPSC.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who appoints the Chairman and members of a State Public Service Commission?","options":["The President","The Governor of the State","The Chief Minister","The Chief Justice of the High Court"],"correct_answer":"The Governor of the State","explanation":"Under Article 316, the Chairman and other members of a State Public Service Commission are appointed by the Governor of the State.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who has the power to remove the Chairman or members of a State Public Service Commission?","options":["The Governor of the State","The President of India","The State Legislature","The Chief Justice of the Supreme Court"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"Although appointed by the Governor, the Chairman and members of SPSC can be removed ONLY by the President of India on grounds of proved misbehaviour after a Supreme Court inquiry (Article 317).","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the term of office for the Chairman and members of an SPSC?","options":["5 years or up to the age of 60 years","6 years or up to the age of 62 years","6 years or up to the age of 65 years","5 years or up to the age of 65 years"],"correct_answer":"6 years or up to the age of 62 years","explanation":"A member of an SPSC holds office for a term of 6 years or until they attain the age of 62 years, whichever is earlier. (For UPSC, the retirement age is 65 years).","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Amendment raised the retirement age of SPSC members from 60 to 62 years?","options":["41st Amendment Act, 1976","42nd Amendment Act, 1976","44th Amendment Act, 1978","61st Amendment Act, 1989"],"correct_answer":"41st Amendment Act, 1976","explanation":"The 41st Constitutional Amendment Act (1976) raised the retirement age of the Chairman and members of SPSC and Joint PSC from 60 to 62 years.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the Chairman of a State PSC be re-appointed to the same post upon retirement?","options":["Yes, for one more term","No, they are ineligible for re-appointment to that office","Yes, if the Governor approves","Yes, if the President approves"],"correct_answer":"No, they are ineligible for re-appointment to that office","explanation":"Article 316(3) explicitly states that a person who holds office as a Chairman or member of a Public Service Commission shall on the expiration of their term be ineligible for re-appointment to that office.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"After retirement, the Chairman of an SPSC is eligible for appointment as:","options":["Chairman or Member of UPSC, or Chairman of any other SPSC","Member of the same SPSC","Any government post in the central government","Any government post in the state government"],"correct_answer":"Chairman or Member of UPSC, or Chairman of any other SPSC","explanation":"A retired SPSC Chairman is eligible for appointment as the Chairman/Member of UPSC or the Chairman of any other SPSC, but not for any other employment under the state or central governments.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"The salaries, allowances, and pensions of the SPSC Chairman and members are charged on the:","options":["Consolidated Fund of India","Consolidated Fund of the State","Contingency Fund of the State","Public Account of the State"],"correct_answer":"Consolidated Fund of the State","explanation":"Under Article 322, the expenses of the SPSC, including salaries and pensions of its members, are charged on the Consolidated Fund of the State and are thus not subject to vote by the State Legislature.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"To whom does the SPSC present its annual performance report?","options":["The President","The Governor","The Chief Minister","The State Legislature directly"],"correct_answer":"The Governor","explanation":"Article 323 requires the SPSC to present annually to the Governor of the State a report as to the work done by the Commission. The Governor then places it before the state legislature.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following bodies is created by an act of Parliament as a statutory body, rather than a constitutional body?","options":["UPSC","SPSC","Joint State Public Service Commission (JSPSC)","Election Commission"],"correct_answer":"Joint State Public Service Commission (JSPSC)","explanation":"While UPSC and SPSC are created directly by the Constitution, a JSPSC is created by an Act of Parliament on the request of the state legislatures concerned, making it a statutory body.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who appoints the Chairman and members of a Joint State Public Service Commission (JSPSC)?","options":["The Governors of the concerned States jointly","The President of India","The Union Public Service Commission","The Parliament"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"The Chairman and members of a JSPSC are appointed by the President of India, not by the Governors of the associated states.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Governor can suspend the Chairman or a member of the SPSC when:","options":["A political change happens in the state","An inquiry is pending against them in the Supreme Court for removal","They are absent from work for 15 days","The Chief Minister recommends so"],"correct_answer":"An inquiry is pending against them in the Supreme Court for removal","explanation":"While only the President can remove them, the Governor of the state can suspend the Chairman or member of an SPSC pending the President's final order based on the Supreme Court's inquiry report.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the required proportion of members in SPSC who must have held government office?","options":["At least one-third","At least one-half","At least two-thirds","The Constitution specifies no statutory proportion"],"correct_answer":"At least one-half","explanation":"Article 316 specifies that as nearly as may be one-half of the members of every Public Service Commission shall be persons who have held office for at least 10 years either under the GoI or a State Government.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"For which class of services does the SPSC conduct examinations?","options":["All India Services","Central Civil Services","State Civil Services","Panchayat level services"],"correct_answer":"State Civil Services","explanation":"The SPSC is responsible for conducting examinations for appointments to the services of the State (like SDM, DSP, etc. within the state framework).","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"A member of an SPSC (but not the Chairman) on ceasing to hold office is eligible for appointment as:","options":["A minister in the state government","The Chairman of that SPSC, or Chairman/Member of UPSC or another SPSC","Any post under the Government of India","Ambassador to a foreign nation"],"correct_answer":"The Chairman of that SPSC, or Chairman/Member of UPSC or another SPSC","explanation":"A member of an SPSC is eligible for appointment as the Chairman of that same SPSC, the Chairman of any other SPSC, or the Chairman/Member of the UPSC.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"By whom can the conditions of service of the Chairman and members of SPSC be determined?","options":["The President","The Parliament","The Governor","The State Legislature"],"correct_answer":"The Governor","explanation":"Article 318 empowers the Governor of the State (and the President in the case of UPSC/JSPSC) to determine the number of members of the Commission and their conditions of service.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the conditions of service of an SPSC member be varied to their disadvantage after appointment?","options":["Yes, during a financial emergency","Yes, by a resolution of the State Legislature","No, it is constitutionally prohibited","Yes, by the Governor's special order"],"correct_answer":"No, it is constitutionally prohibited","explanation":"The proviso to Article 318 clearly states that the conditions of service of a member of a Public Service Commission shall not be varied to his disadvantage after his appointment.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"In matters of disciplinary action against a state civil servant, the SPSC acts as:","options":["An executive deciding authority","An advisory body","A judicial court","A legislative drafting body"],"correct_answer":"An advisory body","explanation":"The SPSC's role is advisory. It must be consulted on disciplinary matters affecting a person serving under the state government, but its advice is not binding on the government.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Under what condition is the Chairman of an SPSC deemed guilty of 'misbehaviour' for removal?","options":["They disagree with the Chief Minister","They become interested in any government contract or agreement","They refuse to conduct an examination","They grant an interview to the press"],"correct_answer":"They become interested in any government contract or agreement","explanation":"Article 317(3) defines that a Chairman/member is deemed guilty of misbehaviour if they are concerned/interested in any government contract or participate in its profit from a corporate entity.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Is it mandatory for the state government to consult the SPSC while making reservations for backward classes?","options":["Yes, absolutely mandatory","No, Article 320(4) exempts it","Yes, but only for SC/ST, not OBCs","Yes, if directed by the High Court"],"correct_answer":"No, Article 320(4) exempts it","explanation":"Article 320(4) explicitly states that the Public Service Commission need NOT be consulted in respect of the manner in which any provision for reservations is to be made under Article 16(4).","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is an obligatory consultation area for SPSC?","options":["Methods of recruitment to civil services and posts","Selection for group D posts","Appointing the Advocate General of the State","Nominating members to the State Legislative Council"],"correct_answer":"Methods of recruitment to civil services and posts","explanation":"Under Article 320(3), it is obligatory to consult the SPSC on all matters relating to methods of recruitment to civil services and for civil posts.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"If the State Legislature passes a law extending the functions of the SPSC, can the SPSC take up those new functions?","options":["No, its functions are fixed by the Constitution","Yes, under Article 321","Yes, but only with President's prior approval","No, only Parliament can extend SPSC functions"],"correct_answer":"Yes, under Article 321","explanation":"Article 321 empowers the State Legislature to enact a law to extend the functions of the State Public Service Commission regarding services of the State, local authorities, or any PSUs in the state.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"The SPSC presents a memorandum to the Governor explaining cases where its advice was not accepted. Where does this memorandum ultimately go?","options":["To the President of India","To the State Legislature","To the High Court","It is kept confidential within the Governor's office"],"correct_answer":"To the State Legislature","explanation":"The Governor MUST place the SPSC's annual report along with the memorandum detailing the reasons for non-acceptance of SPSC advice before the State Legislature.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"In 1966, an act of Parliament created a Joint State Public Service Commission for which two states?","options":["Maharashtra and Gujarat","Punjab and Haryana","Andhra Pradesh and Telangana","Bihar and Jharkhand"],"correct_answer":"Punjab and Haryana","explanation":"The Punjab Reorganisation Act, 1966, created a Joint Public Service Commission for the states of Punjab and Haryana.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"How does the President act upon receiving the Supreme Court's report recommending the removal of an SPSC member?","options":["The President may ignore it","The President must act according to the SC advice, it is binding","The President sends it to the state Governor for final decision","The President sends it to Parliament for voting"],"correct_answer":"The President must act according to the SC advice, it is binding","explanation":"In cases of removal for 'misbehaviour', the President refers the matter to the Supreme Court. The advice tendered by the Supreme Court after inquiry is binding on the President.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can an SPSC perform the needs of any other State without forming a Joint Commission?","options":["No, a Joint Commission must be formed","Yes, if requested by the Governor of that state and approved by the President","Yes, if the Chief Ministers mutually agree","Yes, if Parliament passes a resolution"],"correct_answer":"Yes, if requested by the Governor of that state and approved by the President","explanation":"Under Article 315(2), with the approval of the President, a State PSC can agree to serve the needs of another state if requested by the Governor of that other state.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"The provision protecting SPSC members from arbitrary removal is aimed at:","options":["Maintaining their subordination to the Governor","Ensuring they do not enter politics","Guaranteeing structural independence and impartiality","Saving state expenditure"],"correct_answer":"Guaranteeing structural independence and impartiality","explanation":"The strict removal process (by the President after SC inquiry) ensures security of tenure, allowing the SPSC to function independently without fear of executive reprisal.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"A member of an SPSC can resign from their office by addressing the resignation to:","options":["The President of India","The Governor of the State","The Chairman of the SPSC","The Chief Justice of the State High Court"],"correct_answer":"The Governor of the State","explanation":"Although SPSC members are removed by the President, their resignation letters are addressed to the appointing authority — the Governor of the State.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"If the office of the Chairman of SPSC becomes vacant, an acting Chairman can be appointed by:","options":["The President","The Governor","The senior-most member assumes it automatically","The Chief Minister"],"correct_answer":"The Governor","explanation":"Under Article 316(1A), the Governor of the State is empowered to appoint one of the members of the SPSC as an acting Chairman when the office becomes vacant or the Chairman is absent.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},
    {"text":"The jurisdiction of the SPSC does NOT extend to:","options":["Appointments to posts in the state secretariat","Recruitment to state police services","Appointments to the subordinate judiciary (other than District Judges)","Group D (Class IV) employees of the state"],"correct_answer":"Group D (Class IV) employees of the state","explanation":"SPSC primarily deals with Group A and Group B (gazetted) services of the state. Lower-level recruitment (Group C and D) is usually handled by subordinate selection boards or departmental committees.","chapter_number":50,"level":1},

    # CH50 SPSC L2 (+20)
    {"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the SPSC:\n1. The Constitution has not specified the strength of the SPSC.\n2. The Constitution has not prescribed any specific educational qualifications for being appointed as a member of the SPSC.\n3. Half of the members of SPSC must have held government office for at least 5 years.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because the Constitution requires half the members to have held government office for at least 10 years, not 5. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the removal process of a member of the SPSC:\n1. The Governor has no power to remove an SPSC member.\n2. The President can remove them on grounds of insolvency or infirmity of mind or body without referring the matter to the Supreme Court.\n3. The President must refer the matter to the Supreme Court only if the removal is on the ground of 'misbehaviour'.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The Governor only suspends; President removes. For 'misbehaviour', SC inquiry is mandatory. For insolvency, infirmity, or engaging in paid employment, the President can remove directly without SC inquiry.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following regarding SPSC regulations:\n1. The Governor can make regulations specifying the matters in which it shall not be necessary for the SPSC to be consulted.\n2. All such regulations made by the Governor must be laid before the State Legislature for at least 14 days.\n3. The State Legislature has the power to amend or repeal these regulations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All are correct. Proviso to Article 320 empowers the Governor to make regulations excluding matters from SPSC consultation, but these must be laid before the legislature (usually for 14 days) which can amend or repeal them.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Joint State Public Service Commission (JSPSC):\n1. It is a constitutional body explicitly created under Article 315.\n2. Its members are appointed by the President, not the Governors.\n3. It presents its annual report to the President of India.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect: JSPSC is created by an Act of Parliament (statutory body). Statement 3 is incorrect: JSPSC presents its annual report to the Governors of the respective states concerned, who place it before their legislatures. Only 2 is correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following limitations on the SPSC:\n1. It is not consulted while making reservations of appointments in favour of backward classes.\n2. It is not consulted while taking into consideration the claims of SCs and STs in making appointments to services.\n3. The SPSC's advice is binding on the State Government in matters of disciplinary actions.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because the advice of SPSC is advisory in nature, not binding on the State Government. Statements 1 and 2 correctly map constitutional exemptions under Article 320(4) and 335.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following comparing SPSC and UPSC:\n1. Both SPSC and UPSC members have a tenure of 6 years but different retirement ages (62 for SPSC, 65 for UPSC).\n2. Both SPSC and UPSC members submit their resignations to the President.\n3. Both are considered the primary central recruiting agencies for their respective jurisdictions.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect because while UPSC members resign to the President, SPSC members address their resignations to the Governor of the State. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"According to the Supreme Court's jurisprudence on SPSC appointments and actions:\n1. If the government fails to consult the SPSC where it is obligatory under Article 320, the resulting appointment is automatically null and void.\n2. The provision regarding consultation with SPSC is directory and not mandatory.\n3. A civil servant cannot claim a legal right or remedy in court solely because the SPSC was not consulted before disciplinary action was taken against him.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The SC has ruled that Article 320 provisions regarding consultation are directory (not mandatory). Non-consultation does not invalidate the action, nor does it confer an actionable legal right to a civil servant (State of UP vs. Manbodhan Lal Srivastava, 1957).","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following regarding SPSC members after ceasing to hold office:\n1. An SPSC member can be appointed as the Chairman of the UPSC.\n2. The Chairman of an SPSC can be appointed as the Governor of a state.\n3. An SPSC member can continue private legal or medical practice while holding office.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect as holding office of profit or engaging in paid employment outside duties of office is a ground for removal. Statement 1 is correct (Art 319). Statement 2 is correct because the office of Governor is not considered an 'employment under the government' in the context of Article 319 (as per SC rulings like Hargovind Pant case).","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"With reference to the State Vigilance Commission (SVC) and the SPSC:\n1. In disciplinary matters, the State government sometimes consults both the SPSC and the SVC.\n2. The advice of the SPSC has precedence over that of the SVC because SPSC is a constitutional body.\n3. Both SPSC and SVC are constitutional bodies created under Part XIV.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because SVC is not a constitutional body. Statements 1 and 2 are correct: SPSC's advice holds more weight due to its independent constitutional status, whereas SVC is an executive/statutory creation to advise on corruption.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following functions of the SPSC:\n1. It conducts examinations for appointments to the services of the state.\n2. It assists two or more states in framing and operating schemes of joint recruitment if requested.\n3. It is consulted on claims for the award of a pension in respect of injuries sustained by a person while serving the state government.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 refers to a function specifically given to the UPSC (under Article 320(2)) regarding assisting states with special qualifications. SPSC does not assist other states in this manner. Statements 1 and 3 are correct functions under Article 320.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the SPSC's role in the promotion of officers:\n1. SPSC must be consulted on the principles to be followed in making promotions and transfers from one service to another.\n2. SPSC must be consulted on the suitability of candidates for such promotions and transfers.\n3. Departmental Promotion Committees (DPCs) in the state normally have an SPSC member or Chairman presiding over them.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. Under Article 320, SPSC is consulted on both the principles of promotion/transfer and the suitability of candidates. In practice, a member of the SPSC presides over the state's DPC to ensure fairness and merit.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider differences between SPSC and UPSC:\n1. UPSC submits its report to the President, SPSC to the Governor.\n2. UPSC members are removed by the President, SPSC members are removed by the Governor.\n3. Jurisdiction of UPSC can be extended by Parliament, while jurisdiction of SPSC can be extended by the State Legislature.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect because both UPSC and SPSC members are removed ONLY by the President of India. Statements 1 and 3 represent accurate differences.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"If an SPSC member acts as an Acting Chairman, when does their acting term end?\n1. When the regular Chairman enters upon his office.\n2. When the acting member reaches the age of 62 years.\n3. When the President issues a notification ending the acting term.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Under Article 316(1A), an acting Chairman holds office until a person appointed as regular Chairman enters upon the duties, OR until the acting person ceases to hold office as a member (e.g., reaching 62 years or completing 6 years). The President doesn't issue a notification to end it.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the relationship between SPSC and local authorities:\n1. Initially, local authorities (municipalities, panchayats) do not fall under SPSC jurisdiction.\n2. The Governor by order can place local authority recruitment under SPSC.\n3. The State Legislature must pass an Act to extend SPSC's functions to local authorities or public sector corporations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. To extend the functions of the SPSC to respect local authorities, corporate bodies, or public institutions, an Act by the State Legislature is required under Article 321, not just a Governor's order.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"In which of the following cases did the Supreme Court emphasize that the SPSC must be free from political influence?\n1. Ram Kumar Kashyap vs Union of India\n2. Ashok Kumar Yadav vs State of Haryana\n3. L. Chandra Kumar vs Union of India\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"The Ashok Kumar Yadav vs State of Haryana (1985) case, which dealt with Haryana PSC interviews, is a landmark SC judgment emphasizing that PSCs must function with absolute independence and without political pressure or nepotism.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"When an SPSC member is suspended pending inquiry:\n1. The suspension is ordered by the Governor.\n2. During suspension, the member stops receiving their salary.\n3. The suspension lasts until the President passes final orders upon receiving the SC report.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect because suspension does not automatically strip a constitutional functionary of their salary/subsistence allowance unless specific service rules dictate otherwise, and their conditions of service cannot be varied to their disadvantage. Statements 1 and 3 align with Article 317(2).","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'memorandum of non-acceptance' regarding SPSC advice:\n1. It is a tool for ensuring executive accountability to the legislature.\n2. The state government must provide valid reasons in this memorandum for rejecting SPSC advice.\n3. The SPSC can challenge the non-acceptance in the High Court.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The SPSC is an advisory body and does not litigate against the government for rejecting its advice. Statements 1 and 2 are true; the memorandum ensures transparency and holds the executive answerable to the State Legislature.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the disqualifications for appointment to SPSC:\n1. An active politician is legally barred from being appointed to the SPSC.\n2. 50% of the members MUST be from government service backgrounds.\n3. The Constitution prevents the appointment of a sitting civil servant to SPSC.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statements 1 and 3 are incorrect. There is no explicit constitutional bar on politicians (though it violates the spirit of independence), and sitting civil servants are frequently appointed to PSCs. Statement 2 is a constitutional mandate (as nearly as may be half).","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Judicial Services and SPSC (Article 234):\n1. Appointments of District Judges are made on the recommendation of the SPSC alone.\n2. Appointments to the state judicial service (other than District Judges) are made by the Governor after consulting the SPSC and the High Court.\n3. SPSC conducts the examination for the lower judicial services in most states.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect because District Judges are appointed by the Governor in consultation with the High Court (Article 233), without involving SPSC. Statements 2 and 3 are correct regarding lower judicial services (Article 234).","chapter_number":50,"level":2},
    {"text":"What happens if the State Legislature passes a resolution seeking the removal of the SPSC Chairman?\n1. The Governor must remove him immediately.\n2. The President must refer the resolution to the Supreme Court.\n3. The resolution has no legal force for removal, as the constitutional procedure involves the President and the Supreme Court.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"3 only","explanation":"Removal of an SPSC member is strictly governed by Article 317. A legislative resolution cannot bypass the constitutional requirement of Presidential removal based on a Supreme Court inquiry for misbehaviour. Hence, it has no legal binding force.","chapter_number":50,"level":2},

    # CH50 SPSC L3 (+24)
    {"text":"Consider the following about the constitutional history of State Public Service Commissions:\n1. The Government of India Act 1935 originally provided for the establishment of not only a Federal PSC but also Provincial PSCs.\n2. The Constituent Assembly debated whether SPSC members should be removed by the Governor or President, ultimately choosing the President to ensure independence from state politics.\n3. Dr. B.R. Ambedkar strongly opposed the creation of SPSCs, arguing that a single UPSC would unify the country.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; while maintaining a strong centre, the Assembly agreed to Provincial/State PSCs recognizing the large administrative needs of the states. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect historical facts regarding GOI Act 1935 and Constituent Assembly debates to secure independence.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"In the context of SC rulings on the functioning of SPSC, consider the following:\n1. In matters of valuation of answer sheets, the courts usually do not interfere unless there is a material error apparent on the face of the record.\n2. The SC has ruled that SPSC has no inherent power to cancel an examination without prior approval of the state government.\n3. SPSC is subject to the Right to Information Act, and candidates can seek their evaluated answer sheets.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; SPSC, being an independent constitutional body, has the inherent authority to cancel exams if it detects mass copying or leaks, without needing government approval. Statements 1 and 3 are correct (SC rulings established RTI access to answer sheets).","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the procedural requirements for SPSC in disciplinary cases:\n1. Article 311 protections for civil servants demand that the SPSC must be consulted at the stage of framing charges.\n2. Disciplinary authority is not bound to accept the SPSC's quantum of punishment.\n3. If the State Government disagrees with the SPSC on a disciplinary matter, the final decision lies with the State Government.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; consultation with SPSC usually occurs before passing the final order of penalty, not necessarily at the initial stage of framing charges. Statements 2 and 3 correctly highlight the advisory nature of SPSC's recommendations.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"With respect to the principle of 'holding office of profit' causing removal under Article 317:\n1. If a SPSC Chairman is appointed to a government committee with a daily sitting fee, it may be construed as prohibited paid employment.\n2. The objective of barring 'paid employment' is to prevent conflict of interest and executive inducement.\n3. The President requires a Supreme Court inquiry to determine if someone has accepted paid employment under Article 317(1)(b).\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Under Article 317(2), the President can remove a member directly (without SC inquiry) if they are adjudged insolvent, engage in paid employment, or suffer from infirmity. SC inquiry is only required for 'misbehaviour'. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the impact of the 41st Amendment Act (1976) on SPSC:\n1. It increased the retirement age of SPSC members from 60 to 62 years.\n2. The Statement of Objects and Reasons cited that university teachers and advocates generally retire at 60+, making it hard to recruit talent to SPSC at age 60.\n3. It equated the retirement age of SPSC members with that of High Court judges at the time.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The 41st Amendment raised the age to 62, specifically citing that an age limit of 60 made it difficult to attract experienced persons like university professors or lawyers, and it brought parity with High Court judges' retirement age (which was raised to 62 earlier in 1963 by the 15th amendment).","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the phrase 'as nearly as may be one-half' in Article 316 concerning SPSC members:\n1. The phrase is a mere directory instruction; non-compliance does not render the SPSC's constitution invalid.\n2. 'Held office' means holding office strictly under the State Government where the SPSC is situated.\n3. The 10-year experience requirement ensures administrative expertise within the Commission.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; the 10 years of office can be under the Government of India or the Government of ANY State, not just the state in question. Statements 1 and 3 are correct (courts have held 'as nearly as may be' allows some flexibility, not invalidating the body).","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"A State Government decides to bypass the SPSC and recruit gazetted officers via an ad-hoc Departmental Committee. Consider the legal consequences:\n1. The appointments are constitutionally void strictly because SPSC consultation was bypassed.\n2. The legislature can subsequently validate these appointments through an Act.\n3. If challenged, courts will evaluate the policy under Article 14 and 16, rather than strictly invalidating based solely on Article 320(3).\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect because Article 320(3) forms a directory provision. Mere non-consultation doesn't void the appointments automatically. However, bypassing SPSC often faces scrutiny under Articles 14 and 16 (equality of opportunity). The legislature can validate them (Statements 2 and 3 are correct).","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between SPSC's role and the State Election Commission's (SEC) role:\n1. Both SPSC and SEC members are appointed by the Governor.\n2. Both SPSC and SEC members are removed in the same manner as a High Court judge.\n3. While SEC's mandate is restricted to Part IX and IXA bodies, SPSC's mandate covers Part XIV services.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. SEC members are removed in the manner of a High Court Judge. SPSC members are removed by the President based on a Supreme Court inquiry (the procedure is different from the impeachment of a judge). Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"In the context of 'acting Chairman' of an SPSC (Article 316(1A)):\n1. The provision for acting Chairman was added by the 15th Amendment Act (1963) to cure practical difficulties when the office falls vacant.\n2. An acting Chairman has reduced constitutional powers compared to a regular Chairman.\n3. A person can remain an acting Chairman indefinitely until they reach the age of 62.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; an acting Chairman exercises all the powers and duties of the regular Chairman. The 15th Amendment (1963) did add this provision (Statement 1). The acting tenure lasts until a regular appointment is made or they retire (Statement 3).","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Match the constitutional provisions with their implications regarding SPSC's independence:\n1. Article 322 (Expenses charged on Consolidated Fund) -> Prevents financial strangulation by State legislature.\n2. Article 318 proviso (Conditions of service not varied to disadvantage) -> Protects against punitive executive action post-appointment.\n3. Article 319 (Prohibition on subsequent employment) -> Prevents seeking post-retirement favours from the State.\nWhich of the matches are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All matches correctly identify the constitutional safeguards built around the SPSC to insulate members from financial, executive, and post-retirement enticements or pressures.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Which of the following scenarios constitutes 'misbehaviour' under Article 317 leading to the removal of an SPSC member?\n1. Making a mathematical error in tabulating examination results.\n2. Awarding a government catering contract for SPSC to a firm where the member's spouse is the primary shareholder.\n3. Disagreeing with the state cabinet's personnel policy recommendations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Misbehaviour specifically covers being interested/participating in the profit of a government contract (Art 317(3)). Math errors are administrative lapses, and disagreeing with the cabinet is fulfilling advisory independence. Only scenario 2 constitutes constitutional misbehaviour.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'immunity' of SPSC recommendations:\n1. A candidate whose name is placed on the SPSC's recommended merit list acquires an absolute legal right to appointment.\n2. The State Government can refuse to appoint a recommended candidate for valid administrative reasons.\n3. The Supreme Court has ruled that the State cannot act arbitrarily in rejecting SPSC's recommended list.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is a well-settled misconception; inclusion in a merit/select list does not confer an indefeasible right to appointment (Shankarsan Dash vs. UoI). However, the state cannot act arbitrarily/mala fide (Statements 2 and 3 are correct).","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the Joint State Public Service Commission (JSPSC):\n1. It is created by a resolution passed by the state legislatures concerned followed by an Act of Parliament.\n2. JSPSC members present their resignation to the Governors of the associated states jointly.\n3. A JSPSC Chairman can subsequently be appointed as UPSC Chairman.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; since the President appoints them, JSPSC members address their resignation to the President of India, not the Governors. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the role of SPSC in framing recruitment rules versus making appointments:\n1. Framing recruitment rules is a legislative/executive policy function where SPSC must be consulted.\n2. Actually making the appointment order is an executive function of the State Government, not the SPSC.\n3. SPSC issues the final appointment letters to the selected candidates.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. SPSC is an recommending body. It sends the list of selected candidates to the relevant government department (e.g., General Administration Dept), which issues the actual appointment letters. Statements 1 and 2 correctly differentiate the roles.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"An activist challenges an SPSC interview process alleging that awarding 40% weightage to the viva-voce is arbitrary. Based on Supreme Court precedents (e.g., Ajay Hasia case):\n1. The SC will likely strike down a 40% interview weightage as excessive and violative of Article 14.\n2. The SC holds that high interview weightage allows for subjective bias and nepotism.\n3. The SC prefers written exams to have significantly higher weightage than interviews for public service selection.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. In Ajay Hasia vs. Khalid Mujib (1981) and subsequent cases, the SC has restricted excessive weightage to interviews (suggesting 12-15% as reasonable for most services), arguing that high percentages lead to arbitrariness and violate Article 14's equality mandate.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"If an SPSC member seeks to contest Lok Sabha elections after retiring at age 62:\n1. They are constitutionally barred from contesting elections under Article 319.\n2. Article 319 only bars 'employment' under the Government, not contesting democratic elections.\n3. A Lok Sabha MP is not an 'officer under the government of India' for the purpose of Article 319.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Article 319 prohibits further *employment* (executive positions, civil posts), but elected political offices (MP/MLA) are not considered 'employment under the government'. Statements 2 and 3 correctly map the legal interpretation.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the interaction between SPSC and the State Legislature:\n1. The Legislature can debate SPSC's advice when the Governor tables the Annual Report.\n2. The Legislature can pass a vote of no-confidence against the SPSC Chairman.\n3. The Legislature can enact a law requiring SPSC to conduct exams for large municipal corporations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; the Legislature has no mechanism to pass a no-confidence motion against an independent constitutional body or its Chairman. Statements 1 (debating the report) and 3 (expanding functions via Article 321) are correct.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Advisory Jurisdiction' analogy for SPSC:\n1. Like the SC's advisory jurisdiction to the President, SPSC's advice to the Governor is not strictly binding.\n2. However, unlike SC advice, the government's rejection of SPSC advice must be explained in writing to the legislature.\n3. SPSC can refuse to give advice to the State Government on matters expressly mentioned in Article 320.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; Article 320 states \"it shall be the duty\" of the SPSC to advise on the listed matters, meaning they cannot capriciously refuse. Statements 1 and 2 are accurate constitutional comparisons.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Does the SC hold original jurisdiction in cases comparing state civil servants' seniority, where SPSC made the original list?\n1. No, such cases primarily go to the State Administrative Tribunal (if existing) or the High Court.\n2. Yes, because SPSC is a constitutional body, all its lists are directly challengeable in SC under Article 131.\n3. The SPSC's merit ranking is considered final and cannot be challenged in any court.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; Article 131 is for federal disputes (Centre vs States), not service disputes. Statement 3 is incorrect; merit lists can be challenged under writ jurisdiction (Art 226/32). Statement 1 is correct; SATs or HCs handle these service matters initially.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the role of the State Government's 'Rules of Business' concerning SPSC:\n1. The Rules of Business may dictate that rejection of SPSC's advice requires the approval of the State Cabinet.\n2. This ensures that an individual minister/secretary cannot arbitrarily override the constitutional body.\n3. The Rules of Business can authorize the Chief Minister to unilaterally suspend the SPSC Chairman.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; suspension under Article 317(2) is a specific constitutional power exercised by the Governor (on advice of the CoM) pending SC inquiry. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect how executive rules ensure respectful treatment of SPSC advice.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the 'independence' of the SPSC, critics often point to which structural vulnerabilities?\n1. The constitution leaves the number of members to the Governor's discretion, allowing possible ‘packing’ of the commission.\n2. State governments often delay placing the SPSC's annual report before the legislature to avoid scrutiny of non-acceptance of advice.\n3. The Constitution does not specify the qualifications of the Chairman, allowing political appointees.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements identify recognized vulnerabilities: 'Packing' (as no fixed number in Constitution), delayed tabling of reports, and lack of specified qualifications for the Chairman (allowing political rather than merit assignments) are all critiques raised by administrative reform commissions.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Consultation' regarding reservation (Article 320(4)):\n1. This provision was specifically designed to keep the sensitive political policy of reservation outside the technocratic purview of the SPSC.\n2. It implies the SPSC cannot invalidate a state government's reservation roster.\n3. It means the SPSC conducts separate exams for reserved and unreserved candidates.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; the SPSC conducts a single combined exam, producing merit lists based on the government's reservation roster. Statements 1 and 2 correctly identify the policy firewall between SPSC technocracy and legislative reservation policy.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"In cases of disciplinary action (Article 320(3)(c)), if a state government reduces the rank of an officer without consulting SPSC:\n1. The officer can claim the order is fundamentally invalid in a court of law.\n2. The SC has ruled that the order remains valid despite the procedural irregularity of not consulting SPSC.\n3. The Governor can subsequently ratify the order to cure the defect.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect because the SC (in State of UP v Manbodhan Lal Srivastava) held that Article 320(3) is directory. The omission of consultation doesn't invalidate the disciplinary penalty, though it is a serious administrative irregularity that the government can later regularize/ratify.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the intersection of Article 311 (Dismissal of Civil Servants) and Article 320 (SPSC Consultation):\n1. Article 311 gives a civil servant the right to be heard.\n2. Article 320(3)(c) requires the government to consult SPSC on the disciplinary matter.\n3. If Article 311's hearing is properly conducted, the failure to consult SPSC under Article 320 does not give the servant a cause of action in court.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. Article 311 is a mandatory constitutional protection (causes of action arise if violated). Article 320's consultation is between the executive and SPSC; failure to do so does not give the employee a legal right to challenge the action, provided Article 311 was respected.","chapter_number":50,"level":3},
]
